Unless you just _really_ love pulling out your hair
looking for obscure bugs, _never_ introduce redundant
copies of identical code like that.

      #ifdef NERF_HEALING_MAXHP_BOOSTS
              heal(HowManyPoints, 0);
      #else
              heal(HowmanyPoints, WhatMaxHpBoost);
      #endif

will take you hours to debug if you have an old
compiler that doesn't warn you about undeclared
variable usages.

There's a reason skilled coders put only and exactly
the stuff that changes with the value of the conditioning
variable inside the conditional part of the compilation,
and it is one of the software development "motherhood
issues": say stuff _once_.

It doesn't matter a bit to the compiler that the
resulting code "reads ugly". For a real eye opener,
capture (usually with a -E flag, IIRC) the output
of the pre-compiler, and look at the hideous mess
the compiler gets as input anyway.

